in architecture?", "choice": "Sanitisation is implemented in the Theme/Renderer Plugin (C14). A dedicated security acceptance test with XSS payloads is required before Phase C exit.", "rationale": "K3 is critical severity. Centralising sanitisation in the renderer plugin ensures all output paths are covered regardless of which plugins contribute content. A security gate before Phase C exit prevents unsafe output from reaching integration.", "alternatives_considered": ["Sanitise in each plugin individually: higher risk of gaps.", "Sanitise only at the Markdown parser level: misses content injected by other plugins."], "what_would_change_this_decision": ["If plugins are allowed to emit raw HTML directly to output (bypassing the renderer), per-plugin sanitisation would also be needed."], "evidence_pointers": [{"artifact_ref": "risk_gov_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "K3", "json_pointer": "/risk_register/2", "note": "XSS/content injection — critical severity", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "Client-side XSS or content injection vulnerabilities if Markdown/HTML is rendered unsafely"}]}, {"id": "DEC6", "question": "How to gate acceptance tests across the hybrid milestones?", "choice": "Phase A gates on AT1/AT2/AT3/AT4/AT13 plus plugin-contract smoke test. Phase B gates each plugin independently (AT8/AT9 for backlinks, AT6/AT7 for tags, AT5/AT12 for search). Phase C runs full suite AT1–AT13 end-to-end plus security gate. Phase D adds AT10/AT11 and performance benchmarks.", "rationale": "Incremental gating ensures each phase delivers verified value. Independent plugin gates in Phase B enable parallel development without waiting for full integration. The full suite in Phase C catches integration issues. Phase D covers non-functional requirements.", "alternatives_considered": ["Run full suite at every phase: slower feedback loops during parallel development.", "Gate only at Phase C: risks late discovery of plugin-level defects."], "what_would_change_this_decision": ["If CI infrastructure supports fast full-suite runs, running AT1–AT13 at every phase would be preferable."], "evidence_pointers": [{"artifact_ref": "branch_set_v1", "entity_id": null, "json_pointer": "/branches/1/acceptance_strategy", "note": "Acceptance strategy defined per phase", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "Phase A gates on AT1/AT2/AT3/AT4/AT13 plus a plugin-contract smoke test"}]}, {"id": "DEC7", "question": "How to manage search index scalability in the plugin architecture?", "choice": "Search Index Plugin (C13) supports optional chunked/sharded index artifacts for larger sites. Performance benchmarks in Phase D validate search responsiveness on 100+ notes.", "rationale": "K4 (search index scale) is high severity. Chunking/sharding allows the search index to scale without degrading initial page load. Deferring performance validation to Phase D is acceptable because Phase B validates functional correctness (AT5/AT12) first.", "alternatives_considered": ["Require chunked indexes from the start: adds complexity before scale is confirmed.", "Use Pagefind as the search plugin: would work but introduces a non-JS binary dependency (noted in K18/K19)."], "what_would_change_this_decision": ["If the expected dataset exceeds 1000+ notes, chunked indexing should be mandatory from Phase B.", "If Pagefind is acceptable as a dependency, it could replace the custom search index plugin."], "evidence_pointers": [{"artifact_ref": "risk_gov_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "K4", "json_pointer": "/risk_register/3", "note": "Search index scale/performance risk", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "Client-side search index size/quality/performance may degrade at scale"}, {"artifact_ref": "architecture_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "C13", "json_pointer": "/options/1/components/5", "note": "Search Index Plugin supports optional sharding", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": null}]}], "supplemental_evaluations": [{"title": "Plugin API Design Risk Assessment", "notes": "The plugin-based architecture (O2) introduces K16 as a primary risk. The mitigation strategy is to design a minimal plugin API with schema validation before Phase B. The API surface should be limited to the six lifecycle hooks (init, collectFiles, transform, resolve, render, finalize) and a well-typed shared context object. Plugin ordering is managed by the orchestrator based on declared dependencies. The thin-slice in Phase A serves as the primary validation vehicle for the plugin API — if the file collector and Markdown parser plugins can successfully register, receive hooks, and populate/read the shared context, the API is viable for Phase B parallelisation.", "evidence": [{"artifact_ref": "architecture_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "O2", "json_pointer": "/options/1", "note": "O2 plugin lifecycle and hook definitions", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": null}, {"artifact_ref": "risk_gov_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "K16", "json_pointer": "/risk_register/15", "note": "Plugin instability risk and mitigation", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": null}]}, {"title": "Dependency Management Comparison (O2 vs O3)", "notes": "Branch B2 notes that K18 (dependency management) is lower risk for O2 than O3 because fewer external dependencies are needed. O2 requires a Markdown parsing library and optionally a search indexing library, but avoids coupling to a full SSG framework and its plugin ecosystem. This reduces the surface area for breaking changes from upstream. However, the custom plugin API itself becomes an internal dependency that must be maintained — this is traded off against the external dependency risk.", "evidence": [{"artifact_ref": "branch_set_v1", "entity_id": null, "json_pointer": "/branches/1/risk_posture", "note": "Branch risk posture on K18", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "K18 (dependency management) is lower risk here since fewer external dependencies are needed