 Add deployment-mode tests (non-root basePath) and nested-content fixtures with link/asset checks over the output.", "owner": "Engineering", "status": "planned"}, "acceptance": null}, {"id": "K13", "severity": "medium", "description": "Excessive memory usage or slow build times on large repositories (including inefficient backlink generation) can cause build failures/timeouts in CI or on user machines.", "mitigation": {"text": "Profile CPU/memory usage; stream where possible; avoid loading all contents at once; optimize link-graph/backlink computation; implement caching/incremental indexing where feasible. Provide configurable limits (max file size, exclude globs) and a performance troubleshooting guide.", "owner": "Engineering", "status": "planned"}, "acceptance": null}, {"id": "K14", "severity": "high", "description": "Incorrect incremental rebuild/watch behavior (if implemented) can lead to stale search index/backlinks after edits, renames, or deletes, confusing users.", "mitigation": {"text": "If watch mode is offered, implement a dependency graph so edits trigger recomputation of affected pages, backlinks, and search index. Provide a 'full rebuild' fallback and add tests for edit/rename/delete scenarios.", "owner": "Engineering", "status": "planned"}, "acceptance": null}, {"id": "K15", "severity": "medium", "description": "Cross-browser compatibility and accessibility issues in generated HTML/CSS/JS (including search UI) may lead to runtime errors or reduced usability for some readers (SM6).", "mitigation": {"text": "Define target browsers and test across them. Adopt baseline accessibility guidelines for templates/components (semantic HTML, focus management, ARIA where needed). Add at least one automated audit step (e.g., Lighthouse/axe) plus manual spot checks.", "owner": "Engineering/Design", "status": "planned"}, "acceptance": null}, {"id": "K16", "severity": "medium", "description": "Overly permissive configuration or plugin/theme extensibility (if added) can introduce instability and make outputs hard to support/reproduce.", "mitigation": {"text": "Keep configuration surface minimal initially; validate configuration schemas; version configurations; gate plugin execution behind explicit allowlists and warnings. Provide a safe default preset and a compatibility policy.", "owner": "Product/Engineering", "status": "planned"}, "acceptance": null}, {"id": "K17", "severity": "medium", "description": "Information disclosure via generated artifacts (e.g., including private notes, embedding absolute filesystem paths in HTML/JS/search index).", "mitigation": {"text": "Support exclude patterns (globs) and a 'private' frontmatter flag. Ensure generated output never includes absolute source paths (use relative identifiers only). Emit a build manifest listing included sources for review.", "owner": "Engineering", "status": "planned"}, "acceptance": null}, {"id": "K18", "severity": "high", "description": "Poorly managed third-party dependencies (e.g., Markdown parser, search indexer/SSG plugins) may introduce breaking changes or security vulnerabilities that destabilize the tool.", "mitigation": {"text": "Pin dependency versions and use a lockfile. Establish a dependency update/vetting process including automated vulnerability scanning and regression testing before upgrades.", "owner": "Engineering", "status": "planned"}, "acceptance": null}, {"id": "K19", "severity": "medium", "description": "A complex installation process or system-specific dependencies for the CLI tool can create a high barrier to entry for users.", "mitigation": {"text": "Target a simple installation path (e.g., a single package manager). Document prerequisites clearly; minimize system-specific dependencies; consider producing distributable bundles where feasible.", "owner": "Engineering", "status": "planned"}, "acceptance": null}, {"id": "K20", "severity": "low", "description": "Scope creep from undefined secondary features (e.g., graph views, themes) could divert resources from core goals and delay delivery.", "mitigation": {"text": "Prioritize core features for MVP; defer optional features until after MVP and track them as distinct work items with explicit approval before implementation.", "owner": "Product", "status": "planned"}, "acceptance": null}], "governance": {"failure_modes": [], "tool_policy": {"stage_policies": [{"stage": "Intake", "allowlisted_tools": ["artifact_read", "artifact_write"], "restrictions": [{"tool": "artifact_read", "rule": "provided_artifacts_only", "notes": "Read only the artifacts explicitly provided in inputs/artifact_refs; no external sources."}, {"tool": "artifact_write", "rule": "write_required_outputs_only", "allowed_artifacts": ["audit_spine", "intake_notes"], "notes": "Artifact writes limited to intake governance outputs for traceability."}, {"artifact_write": "canonical_allowed_only_for_declared_types", "notes": "Allow canonical audit-spine artifacts (run_manifest, event_log, stage_handoff)."}], "hitl_triggers": ["Blocking specification decisions are required but missing (Q1 internal link syntax; Q2 tag representation).", "Any request to expand scope beyond the static-site constraint."]}, {"stage": "Generate", "allowlisted_tools": ["artifact_read", "artifact_write"], "restrictions": [{"tool": "artifact_read", "rule": "provided_artifacts_only", "notes": "Do not use external web browsing or non-provided sources."}, {"tool": "artifact_write", "rule": "planning_only_no_execution_claims", "notes": "Drafting/planning only; do not execute code, access network, or claim runtime validation."}, {"tool": "artifact_write", "rule": "no_secrets_or_local_paths", "notes": "Do not include credentials/tokens or absolute filesystem paths in outputs."}], "hitl_triggers": ["Proposed solution requires server-side runtime for browsing/search/tags/backlinks (violates static constraint).", "Proposed Markdown rendering enables raw/unsanitized HTML or unsafe URL schemes without explicit mitigation."]}, {"stage": "Validate", "allowlisted_tools": ["artifact_read", "artifact_write"], "restrictions": [{"tool": "artifact_write", "rule": "validation_outputs_only", "allowed_artifacts": ["validation_notes", "risk_checks"], "notes": "Write only checklists/findings; do not introduce major scope changes during validation."}], "hitl_triggers": ["Security-sensitive findings (e.g., XSS risk) lack a concrete mitigation.", "Performance/scale targets remain undefined while performance-sensitive features are being committed."]}, {"stage": "Review", "allowlisted_tools": ["artifact_read", "artifact_write"], "restrictions": [{"tool": "artifact_write", "rule": "write_required_outputs_only", "allowed_artifacts": ["review_findings", "audit_spine"], "notes": "Review must be able to write findings and update the audit spine."}, {"tool": "artifact_write", "rule": "no_untracked_decisions", "notes": "Any decision affecting link/tag spec, security posture, or hosting assumptions must be logged in review_findings."}, {"artifact_write": "canonical_allowed_only_for_declared_types", "notes": "Allow findings-only canonical artifacts (review_findings.json)."}], "hitl_triggers": ["Any acceptance of high/critical risks without explicit signoff rationale.", "Any change to blocking questions (Q1/Q2) without stakeholder approval."]}, {"stage": "Judge", "allowlisted_tools": ["artifact_read", "artifact_write"], "restrictions": [{"tool": "artifact_write", "rule": "decision_record_only", "allowed_artifacts": ["governance_decision", "risk_acceptance_log"], "notes": "Record accept/mitigate decisions only; no new features introduced."}], "hitl_triggers": ["Any critical risk remains unmitigated.", "Unresolved blocking questions remain (Q1 or Q2)."]}, {"stage": "Freeze", "allowlisted_tools": ["artifact_read", "artifact_write"], "restrictions": [{"tool": "artifact_write", "rule": "freeze_outputs_only", "allowed_artifacts": ["frozen_risk_register", "frozen_tool_policy"], "notes": "Freeze produces locked governance artifacts; edits require a new run."}], "hitl_triggers": ["Any attempt to modify frozen artifacts without a formal change request."]}]}, "hitl_policy": {"when_to_interrupt": ["Blocking specification decisions are required (Q1 internal link syntax; Q2 tag representation) and are not provided.", "Any proposal that violates the static-site constraint (requires a server/database at runtime).", "Any security-sensitive behavior is proposed (raw HTML passthrough, unsanitized rendering, unsafe URL schemes) without an explicit mitigation and reviewer approval.", "Any high/critical risk is proposed for acceptance rather than mitigation, or is accepted without explicit signoff rationale.", "Any ingestion behavior could read outside the intended directory (symlinks/path traversal) without explicit user confirmation.", "On repeated failures to pass critical path test cases.", "Before making significant architectural changes or adding new major dependencies.", "When a generated solution deviates from the defined project capsule requirements.", "Upon detection of a high-severity security vulnerability in code or dependencies."], "approval_roles": ["Product Owner", "Product Manager", "Tech Lead", "Engineering Lead", "Security Reviewer", "Security Analyst"]}}, "decision_log": [{"id": "DEC1", "question": "Which architecture option should be used?", "choice": "O1 — Monolithic single-command CLI with in-memory build pipeline.", "rationale": "Minimises upfront design overhead and delivers the fastest path to a working end-to-end prototype. Simple single-process build is easy to reason about and debug. Thin-slice milestones mitigate extensibility concerns by validating each core goal incrementally.", "alternatives_considered": ["O2 — Custom plugin-based SSG core: rejected as over-engineering for initial scope; plugin API stabilisation would delay delivery.", "O3 — Eleventy-based SSG: rejected to avoid external dependency constraints, upgrade-cycle risks, and reduced control over build internals.", "O4 — Multi-tool pipeline with on-disk intermediates: rejected due to user-experience complexity and orchestration overhead."], "what_would_change_this_decision": ["If the project scope expanded to require third-party plugin extensibility or theme marketplace, O2 would become preferable.", "If an existing SSG already covered 80%+ of requirements with stable plugins, O3 would reduce implementation effort.", "If the team needed to use different languages for different pipeline stages, O4 would be reconsidered."], "evidence_pointers": [{"artifact_ref": "architecture_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "O1", "json_pointer": "/options/0", "note": "Monolithic CLI option definition", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "Simple single-process build; easy to reason about and debug"}, {"artifact_ref": "branch_set_v1", "entity_id": "B1", "json_pointer": "/branches/0/rationale", "note": "Branch rationale selecting O1", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "The monolithic CLI (O1) minimises upfront design overhead"}]}, {"id": "DEC2", "question": "What milestone strategy should be used?", "choice": "Thin-slice incremental delivery across 5 milestones, each producing a shippable increment.", "rationale": "Thin slices allow each core goal (G1–G5) to be validated independently before proceeding. This reduces integration risk and enables early feedback. Each milestone gates on its mapped acceptance tests, ensuring quality at every increment.", "alternatives_considered": ["Layer-cake approach (all parsing first, then all rendering, then all features): rejected because it delays end-to-end validation and increases integration risk.", "Big-bang single milestone: rejected because it provides no intermediate validation points and increases delivery risk."], "what_would_change_this_decision": ["If the project had extremely tight deadlines requiring parallel workstreams, a different decomposition might be needed.", "If requirements were highly interdependent with no natural slicing points, a layer-cake approach might be unavoidable."], "evidence_pointers": [{"artifact_ref": "branch_set_v1", "entity_id": "B1", "json_pointer": "/branches/0/milestone_strategy", "note": "Thin-slice milestone strategy from branch definition", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "Each milestone delivers a shippable increment that can be tested against its acceptance tests"}]}, {"id": "DEC3", "question": "When should blocking questions Q1 (link syntax) and Q2 (tag format) be resolved?", "choice": "Both must be resolved and documented before any coding begins (before M1 work starts).", "rationale": "Q1 and Q2 are blocking questions that affect the core parser (C2), link graph (C3), and tag index (C4). Starting implementation without these decisions would cause rework and risk incorrect extraction logic. The risk posture explicitly prioritises resolving these first, and alignment warnings AL-001/AL-002 flag them.", "alternatives_considered": ["Defer Q1/Q2 and implement a configurable multi-syntax parser: rejected because it increases complexity without clear user need and delays delivery.", "Pick defaults and allow override later: partially acceptable but still requires a documented default before coding."], "what_would_change_this_decision": ["If the user explicitly requested support for multiple link/tag syntaxes simultaneously from day one.", "If a clear industry standard emerged that eliminated ambiguity."], "evidence_pointers": [{"artifact_ref": "capsule_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "Q1", "json_pointer": "/open_questions/0", "note": "Blocking question on link syntax", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "What Markdown flavor and internal link syntax should be supported"}, {"artifact_ref": "capsule_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "Q2", "json_pointer": "/open_questions/1", "note": "Blocking question on tag format", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "How are tags represented in the notes"}, {"artifact_ref": "risk_gov_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "K1", "json_pointer": "/risk_register/0", "note": "Risk of link resolution errors due to ambiguous syntax", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "Link/backlink resolution errors due to ambiguous or mixed internal link syntax"}, {"artifact_ref": "risk_gov_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "K2", "json_pointer": "/risk_register/1", "note": "Risk of tag extraction inconsistency", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "Tag extraction inconsistency due to unspecified or mixed tag formats"}]}, {"id": "DEC4", "question": "How should XSS and content injection risks (K3) be mitigated?", "choice": "Disable raw HTML passthrough and block unsafe URL schemes (e.g., javascript:, data:) in the Markdown renderer from M1 onward. Require explicit opt-in configuration for any raw HTML support.", "rationale": "K3 is rated critical. Readers of the generated static site must be protected from injected scripts. Disabling raw HTML by default is the simplest and most robust mitigation. A security test suite with known XSS payloads is included in M1 deliverables.", "alternatives_considered": ["Allow raw HTML with sanitisation library: more permissive but increases attack surface and dependency risk.", "Allow raw HTML with no sanitisation: unacceptable given critical severity."], "what_would_change_this_decision": ["If the user explicitly requires raw HTML embedding (e.g., for embedded widgets), a sanitisation-based approach with explicit opt-in would be adopted."], "evidence_pointers": [{"artifact_ref": "risk_gov_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "K3", "json_pointer": "/risk_register/2", "note": "Critical XSS risk", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "Client-side XSS or content injection vulnerabilities if Markdown/HTML is rendered unsafely"}]}, {"id": "DEC5", "question": "How should path traversal and unintended file inclusion (K9) be mitigated?", "choice": "Constrain ingestion to the declared root directory, reject symlinks by default, and maintain an allowlist of file extensions (.md only). Reject any paths that escape the root.", "rationale": "K9 is rated high severity. The monolithic parser (C2) must enforce filesystem boundaries to prevent leaking local files into the generated site. This is implemented in the file discovery phase of M1.", "alternatives_considered": ["Follow symlinks with user confirmation: adds complexity and risk; deferred unless explicitly requested.", "No restriction: unacceptable given high severity."], "what_would_change_this_decision": ["If the user's workflow requires symlinked note directories, an explicit opt-in flag with a warning would be added."], "evidence_pointers": [{"artifact_ref": "risk_gov_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "K9", "json_pointer": "/risk_register/8", "note": "Path traversal risk", "candidate_id": null, "artifact_id": null, "quote": "Path traversal or unintended file inclusion during ingestion"}]}, {"id": "DEC6", "question": "How should COULD-priority features (R26–R29) be handled?", "choice": "Explicitly defer all COULD features to post-M5 backlog. Document them as deferred items with scope boundaries in M5 deliverables.", "rationale": "K20 identifies scope creep as a risk. Deferring COULD features keeps the team focused on MUST and SHOULD requirements through M1–M5 and ensures core goals are met before optional enhancements are considered.", "alternatives_considered": ["Include graph view (R26) in M5: rejected as it adds significant UI complexity and is not required for core goals.", "Include theming (R27) in M5: rejected as it is cosmetic and can be layered on later without architectural changes."], "what_would_change_this_decision": ["If the user explicitly prioritises a specific COULD feature (e.g., graph view) as essential for their workflow.", "If M1–M4 complete significantly ahead of schedule, COULD features could be pulled into M5."], "evidence_pointers": [{"artifact_ref": "risk_gov_canonical_v1", "entity_id": "K20", "json